.TH IPC_CALL 2
.SH NAME
ipc_call \- send a message and wait for the reply (SYS_CALL)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long ipc_call(long port, msg_t *m);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ipc_call
sends the message
.I m
to
.I port
and sleeps until the server answers with
.BR ipc_reply (2).
It is synchronous, "rendezvous" style, as in QNX or L4: the kernel never
stores messages, it copies them directly from one process to the other the
moment both sides are ready. If a server thread is waiting, the message is
copied at once and that thread gets this processor; otherwise the caller
waits in the port's queue, in order.
.PP
Sent: the four words
.IR m\->w[] ,
and
.I m\->slen
bytes from
.IR m\->sbuf .
The reply comes back into the same
.BR msg_t :
its words into
.IR m\->w[] ,
its data into
.I m\->rbuf
(as much as
.I m\->rlen
holds; the rest is cut), and
.I m\->rlen
is set to the size received. By convention
.I w[0]
holds the operation going and the result coming back. The server learns
the caller's pid, uid and gid from the kernel, never from the message.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 when the reply came (the server's result is in
.IR m\->w[0] ),
or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOENT
No such port, or its server ended before taking the message: it never
reached the server, so sending it again is always safe.
.TP
.B EPIPE
The server ended after taking the message, or the caller's process is
ending.
.TP
.B EINTR
A signal the process catches came while it waited. The server may still
answer later; that reply goes nowhere.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.IR m ,
its
.I sbuf
or
.I rbuf
is not the caller's memory.
.SH NOTES
.B call_named
in the library wraps this call for named servers: it finds the port again
after an
.B ENOENT
(the server was restarted) and repeats requests that are safe to do twice
after an
.BR EPIPE .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ipc_recv (2),
.BR ipc_reply (2),
.BR port_lookup (2),
.BR ipc (7)
